<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Athens</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Athens is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> that was a leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> of the classical era. It was central to the resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Persian Wars</a>, including the victory at Marathon in 490 BCE. After the closure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoplatonism">Neoplatonist</a> school in Athens in the sixth century, some of its philosophers took refuge at the court of the Sasanian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khosrow-i">Khosrow I</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/athens.jpg" alt="Athens" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrew Parlette · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Athens_Acropolis_at_Daybreak.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Athens spreads across the central plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a>, the Athens Basin, which is bounded by Mount Aigaleo to the west, Mount Parnitha to the north, Mount Pentelicus to the north-east and Mount Hymettus to the east. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saronic-gulf">Saronic Gulf</a> lies to the south-west. Lycabettus is one of the tallest of the many hills of the city. Ancient writers debated whether the city took its name from the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> or she from it, and modern scholars generally agree that the goddess took her name from the city. In the founding myth Athena and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a> competed for patronage of the city, and Athena won by offering the first olive tree.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The oldest known human presence is in the Cave of Schist, dated between the 11th and 7th millennia BCE, and Athens has been inhabited continuously for at least 5,000 years. By 1400 BCE it was an important Mycenaean centre, with a major fortress on the Acropolis. It is not known whether Athens was destroyed around 1200 BCE, as other Mycenaean centres were, and the Athenians always held that they were pure <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionians">Ionians</a>. After about 150 years of economic decline, its richly furnished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> burials show that from 900 BCE it was again one of the leading centres of trade in the region.</p><p>Classical Athens was one of the most powerful <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a> of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and a centre of democracy, the arts, education and philosophy. It influenced the Hellenistic world and, above all, ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Its chronology runs through Roman rule from 86 BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> from 395, the Duchy of Athens from 1205 to 1458, Ottoman rule from 1458 and Greece from 1833. Athens became the capital in 1834, after Nafplion, the provisional capital from 1829.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Athens is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attica">Attica</a> region. The monuments of the Classical age are still visible, the best known being the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthenon">Parthenon</a>, and the city also keeps Roman, Byzantine and a few Ottoman monuments. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acropolis-of-athens">Acropolis of Athens</a> and the medieval Daphni Monastery are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>. Athens hosted the first modern Olympic Games in 1896 and the Summer Olympics again in 2004.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Athens” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its campaigns included Cyrus's conquest of Lydia, where camels were reportedly deployed to unsettle the Lydian cavalry, the conquest of Egypt (525 BCE), Darius's Scythian campaign (c. 513 BCE), and Xerxes's massive invasion of Greece (480 BCE), which brought victory at Thermopylae and the sack of <b>Athens</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 307 BCE his son Demetrius expelled Cassander's governor Demetrius of Phalerum from <b>Athens</b>, and in 306 BCE, after Demetrius destroyed Ptolemy's fleet off Salamis in Cyprus, Antigonus took the title of king and conferred it on his son, the first of the Successors to do so.</blockquote><a href="/empire/antigonid-kingdom-of-macedonia">Antigonid Kingdom of Macedonia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Athens</b> was inhabited continuously from the Neolithic, and in the Late Bronze Age the Acropolis carried a fortified Mycenaean palace.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Resistance was strong where Thebes was feared: Plataea, according to Thucydides, placed itself under Athenian protection, traditionally in 519 BCE, and in 506 BCE the Boeotians and Chalcidians were defeated by <b>Athens</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Demosthenes claimed in 355 BCE that the Bosporus sent <b>Athens</b> about 400,000 medimnoi of grain a year, as much as all its other sources combined; the figure is debated, but the trade was clearly vital to Athens.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The city's position allowed it to levy dues on passing ships, a source of revenue that its rulers, and at times <b>Athens</b>, tried to exploit.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
